Using the dictionary
_Using the dictionary
	 
	[m1][com][b]Headwords[/b]
	 
	 The [b]headword[/b] is the word you look up in a dictionary. Headwords are listed in alphabetical order, and printed in bold type so that they stand out on the page. Each headword may contain other references such as [b]phrases[/b] and [b]compounds[/b], which are in smaller bold type. The two headwords appearing at the top of each page indicate the first and last word dealt with on the page in question.
	 
	[m1][b]Translations[/b]
	 
	 The translations of the headword are printed in ordinary roman type. As a rule, translations separated by a comma can be regarded as interchangeable for the meaning indicated. Translation separated by a semi-colon are not interchangeable, though the different meaning splits are generally marked by an indicator (see below). Where a semi-colon separates translations and the indicator refers to a different part of speech eg. ([i]see adj.[/i]), the translations mirror the splits shown at the other part of speech eg. [b]annul/annulment[/b].
	 
	 It is not always possible to give an exact translation equivalent, for instance when the English word denotes an object or institution which does not exist or exists in a different form in Russia or in the Republics. If an approximate equivalent exists, it is given, preceded by ≈. If there is no cultural equivalent a [i]gloss[/i] is given to explain the source item.
	 
	 [b]Indicators[/b]
	 
	 An [i]indicator[/i] is a piece of information in the source language about the usage of the headword to guide you to the most appropriate translation. Indicators give some idea of the contexts in which the headword might appear, or they provide the synonyms for the headword. They are printed in italic type and shown in brackets.
	 
	 Colloquial and informal language in the dictionary is marked at the headword. You should assume that the translations will match the source language in register and rude or offensive translations are also marked with (!).
	 
	 [b]Pronunciation[/b]
	 
	 On the English-Russian side of the dictionary you will find the phonetic spelling of the word in square brackets after the headword. Where the entry is composed of two or more unhyphenated words, each of which is given elsewhere in this dictionary, you will find the pronunciation of each word in its alphabetical position. A list of the symbols used is given at section Английское произношение.
	 
	 For Russian-English, stress is given on all Russian words as a guide to pronunciation. Where stress can be placed over either of two vowels, the most common or correct stress position is shown for the purpose of this dictionary. Words which are spelt in the same way, but have different stress positions are treated as separate entries, the order following the order of the stress eg. [b]за́мок[/b] comes before [b]замо́к[/b]. The section Russian Pronunciation explains Russian phonetics in more detail.
	 
	 [b]Keywords[/b]
	 
	 In this dictionary we have given special status to “key” Russian and English words. As these words can be grammatically complex and often have many different usages, they have been given special attention in the dictionary, and are labelled with [b]KEYWORD[/b].
	 
	 [b]Abbreviations and proper names[/b]
	 
	 Abbreviation, acronyms and proper names have been included in the word list in alphabetical order.
	 
	 [b]“You” in phrases[/b]
	 
	 In translations of English phrases containing “you/your” or the imperative, “Вы/Ваш” and the formal form is given, unless the phrase is very colloquial eg. “get lost!” where it would be more natural to give the familiar form of the imperative.
	 
	 [b]Use of [i]or/или[/i], oblique and brackets[/b]
	 
	 The words [i]“or”[/i] on the English-Russian side, and [i]“или”[/i] on the Russian-English side are used between interchangeable parts of a translation or source phrase. The oblique (/) is used between non-interchangeable alternatives in the translation of the source phrase. Round brackets are used to show the optional parts of the translation or source phrase.
	 
	 [b]Using of the swung dash (\~)[/b]
	 
	 The swung dash is used on the English-Russian side of the dictionary to stand for the headword in phrases eg. at “[b]order[/b]” the phrase “[b]out of order[/b]” is shown as “[b]out of \~[/b]”. On the Russian-English side of the dictionary the swung dash can either stand for the full headword eg. at “[b]д[']о[/']бр|ый[/b]” the phrase “[b]д[']о[/']брый день[/b]” is shown as “[b]\~ день[/b]”, or it can stand for the part of the word before the hairline eg. at “[b]д[']о[/']бр|ый[/b]” the phrase “[b]д[']о[/']брое [']у[/']тро[/b]” appears as “[b]\~ое [']у[/']тро[/b]”.
	 
	 [b]Use of the superior asterisk (*)[/b]
	 
	 The asterisk (*) is used to mark translations which are in some way irregular in their declension. The Russian-English side of the dictionary contains further information on irregularities.
	 
	 [b]American variants[/b]
	 
	 American spelling variants are generally shown at the British headword eg. [b]colour/color[/b] and also as a separate entry if they are not alphabetical adjacent to the British form. Variant forms are generally shown as headwords in their own right eg. [b]trousers/pants[/b], unless the British and American forms are alphabetically adjacent, in which case the American form is only shown separately if phonetics are required eg. [b]jump leads/jumper cables[/b].
	 
	 [b]Russian reflexive verbs[/b]
	 
	 Russian reflexive verbs eg. [b]м[']ы[/']ться, кр[']а[/']ситься[/b] are listed under the basic verb eg. [b]мыть, кр[']а[/']сить[/b].
